# docstring-closed-early-by-unescaped-quote — the same defect, twice, and now a check

STATUS: DONE in 2.0.14. The 2.0.13 instance was found while editing the
same file; the check was written the same day.

## Symptom

An unescaped double quote inside a docstring closes it early. What
follows is no longer prose — it is READ AS FORMS, and they land in the
function's body.

It has now happened twice, and only one of the two was caught by running
the tests:

  2.0.9   CALL-IN-FILE-COMPILATION-UNIT's docstring contained
          "SPEED 3 compiles the whole file at once". The quotes ended the
          docstring, leaving SPEED 3 compiles the whole file at once as
          bare symbols in the body. SBCL DELETES the dead variable read;
          CCL evaluates it and signals `Unbound variable: SPEED'. Every
          LOAD failed on CCL and nothing failed on SBCL, so it shipped
          and sat there for two releases.

  2.0.13  (SETF FRAME-BINDING)'s docstring was broken by a shell-quoting
          idiom ('"'"') that leaked into the source through the editing
          script. The truncated remainder read as a QUOTED STRING — one
          dead constant at the head of the body. A dead constant upsets
          neither host: all 31 suites passed on SBCL and on CCL, both
          compiler gates passed, and it shipped.

## Why the tests are not a detector for this

Whether a broken docstring is caught depends on what the prose happens to
say. Prose that reads as bare symbols breaks CCL and not SBCL; prose that
reads as a string or a number breaks neither. The 2.0.13 instance was one
character away from being the 2.0.9 instance.

So the tests will keep catching some of these and missing others, and
which ones is decided by the sentence. That is not a test, that is a
coincidence.

## What IS reliable

The symptom is STRUCTURAL, not textual. Whatever the prose says, the
result is dead forms in a definition's body, before its last form. Real
code does not do that — a body form that is a constant, a quoted object
or a bare variable read, in non-final position, cannot have an effect and
cannot be the body's value. A broken docstring always produces one.

## The check

`make check-docstrings` (tools/check-docstrings.lisp) reads every .lisp
file in the tree and reports any DEFUN / DEFMACRO / DEFMETHOD /
DEFINE-COMPILER-MACRO whose body holds such a form before its last.

Two details it needs in order to be trustworthy rather than merely
reassuring:

  - It READS every file, including those whose packages this image does
    not have (CFFI, charms, a fiveam without SKIP). A file it cannot read
    is a file it cannot check, and skipping quietly would make it blind
    exactly where nobody is looking. Missing packages are conjured empty
    from the text, a `PKG:SYM' that is not external takes SBCL's CONTINUE
    restart, and `#.' reads as a placeholder so *READ-EVAL* can stay NIL.
    279 files, none skipped.

  - It was verified RED before being believed: reintroducing the exact
    2.0.13 defect makes it report
    `api.lisp: (SETF FRAME-BINDING): dead form in body: ...' and exit 1.

Wired into both CI lanes.

## The other half: how the quote got in

Both instances came from EDITING, not from typing: a shell-quoting idiom
inside a script that writes Lisp. The check catches the result whatever
the cause, which is the right place to defend — but the cheaper habit is
to use `like this' rather than "like this" inside a docstring, which is
what the surrounding code already does.
